WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.300
Speaker 1: Republicans are increasingly&nbsp;
demanding, suggesting that Ron DeSantis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:11.520
block the extradition of Donald Trump from&nbsp;
Florida to New York. If indeed Donald Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.080
is indicted and arrested in the state of&nbsp;
New York, as many of us believe he might be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.380
I guess although it was supposed to happen&nbsp;
Tuesday and then Wednesday and then it didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:25.140
happen. Put that aside for a moment. More and&nbsp;
more Republicans are making the request that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:30.600
DeSantis block the extradition of Trump to&nbsp;
New York. Can dissenters even do this? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.680
that's a good question. We'll get to that in&nbsp;
a moment. First, here is Florida Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:39.960
Congressman Matt Gaetz making the case. If I were.
Speaker 2: Governor of Florida, I would not allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:47.460
any Floridian to be hauled before some Soros&nbsp;
backed prosecutor in some blue city over politics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:52.260
And I wouldn't make an exception to not protect&nbsp;
the president of the United States. Ron DeSantis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:57.600
should be standing in the breach to stop any&nbsp;
sort of extradition of President Trump from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.580
the state of Florida. And the fact that he's not&nbsp;
doing so puts every Floridian at risk who could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:07.860
be the subject of a false allegation. I know Ron&nbsp;
DeSantis well. He's a friend, but he hasn't been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:13.740
the target of false and persistent allegations&nbsp;
like President Trump has. And so I don't think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:19.380
he really understands the nature of this fight&nbsp;
right now. I think that it was a bit uncouth for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:23.580
him to take a jab at the former president. And&nbsp;
there's a role for the governor of Florida here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:28.800
I think, to stand up for our state, for the&nbsp;
rule of law, and to push back against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:35.460
Soros ization of the criminal justice system.
Speaker 1: So is it actually something that Ron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:42.000
DeSantis can do? I will tell you in a moment,&nbsp;
Charlie Kirk making the exact same case about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.569
this extradition. And everybody is on message with&nbsp;
the Soros stuff, you will notice it doesn't Ron.&nbsp;

00:01:47.569 --> 00:01:52.980
Speaker 2: DeSantis create a sanctuary state for&nbsp;
a former president under political persecution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.180 --> 00:02:00.840
If the left is able to create sanctuary states&nbsp;
for rapists and murderers, thugs and criminals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.460
I don't think it's too big of an ass&nbsp;
for Ron DeSantis to create a sanctuary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:12.589
state for a former president who's being&nbsp;
persecuted. By a Soros funded DEA. Yeah.&nbsp;

00:02:12.589 --> 00:02:18.540
Speaker 1: So it's an interesting legal question.&nbsp;
Can a governor block the extradition of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:24.000
indicted person from their state to the state&nbsp;
in which they are indicted? Can a governor do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:31.500
that? Governors do have the power to block&nbsp;
extradition of a person who is wanted in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:38.400
another state. But in very limited circumstances,&nbsp;
a governor may block the extradition of a person.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:44.160
If the governor determines that the person's&nbsp;
rights would be violated in the extradition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:51.420
or if the governor believes that the request for&nbsp;
extradition is politically motivated or improper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:58.260
Now, that just happens to be exactly what these&nbsp;
right wingers are claiming. They are saying that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:05.040
the the prosecution of Trump, if it happens,&nbsp;
is politically motivated, would be politically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.560
motivated. However, it is not that the governor&nbsp;
just says, yep, it's politically motivated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.780
so there's no extradition. If a governor asserts&nbsp;
that they are blocking extradition from their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:21.360
state to another for that reason, it can be&nbsp;
challenged in court. And ultimately it's up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.340
to courts to determine whether extradition should&nbsp;
be allowed. From everything I've read right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.960
there is every indication that if DeSantis&nbsp;
tried to block Trump's extradition from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:36.780
Florida to New York on these grounds, courts&nbsp;
would say, You can't do it. Also worth noting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:42.720
governors cannot block extradition to another&nbsp;
country that falls under federal jurisdiction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:49.620
So it's a nice little pipe dream that Charlie Kirk&nbsp;
and Matt Gaetz have. But it is very unlikely to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:53.400
what keeps Trump out of the state of New York.&nbsp;
And by the way, Trump's lawyers have already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:58.920
said he would surrender. Will it happen? Will&nbsp;
the arrest happen? That's a different question.

